#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175733 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e09c5b9-5f31-52a5-b696-b2df1879d289" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing a Binomial in One Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "1be25af9-d59f-5270-b9e1-d0c5dcd731e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14143" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac{2}{1-2k}=-\\dfrac{4}{3k}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand $k=0$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{1-2k}&=-\\dfrac{4}{3k} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{2}{1-2k}&=\\dfrac{-4}{3k} \\\\[5pt] 2 \\cdot 3k &= (-4) \\cdot (1-2k) \\\\[5pt] 6k&=-4+8k \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6k&=-4+8k \\\\[5pt] 6k-8k &=-4+8k-8k \\\\[5pt] -2k&=-4 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{-2k}{-2} &= \\dfrac{-4}{-2} \\\\[5pt] k &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $k = 2$ is different from $k=0$ and\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $k=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175734 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175908 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "208596d0-1a45-57b2-afa5-d17e99218e38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "72cf8733-9727-5a9f-8028-1dee62e5715d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14146" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{2}{4x+12}=\\dfrac{2}{x+3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{2}{4x+12} &= \\dfrac{2}{x+3} \\\\ 2 \\cdot (x+3) &= (4x+12) \\cdot 2 \\\\ 2x + 6 &= 8x + 24 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x + 6 &= 8x + 24 \\\\ 2x + 6-24 &= 8x + 24-24 \\\\ 2x-18 &= 8x \\\\ 2x-18-2x &= 8x-2x \\\\ -18 &= 6x \\ \\dfrac{-18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6x}{6} \\\\ -3 &= x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-3$ cannot be a solution, the equation has **no solutions.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175739 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175907 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3e1fc3-c46b-5958-92b2-0f2dc11ff961" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Containing Negative Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "2d5b690c-bee9-5503-8f21-285b8c42d5fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14705" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $-\\dfrac{3}{2x}=\\dfrac{2}{x+7}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ and $x=-7$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7} \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{-3}{2x}&=\\dfrac{2}{x+7} \\\\[5pt] (-3) \\cdot (x+7) &=2x \\cdot (2) \\\\[5pt] -3x - 21&= 4x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3x - 21&= 4x \\\\[5pt] -3x -21 + 3x&=4x + 3x \\\\[5pt] -21&=7x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{-21}{7}&=\\dfrac{7x}{7} \\\\[5pt] -3 &= x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x =-3$ is different from $x=0$ and $x=-7$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x= -3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175908 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "208596d0-1a45-57b2-afa5-d17e99218e38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Binomials in Both Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "219bd34f-9e71-5315-809c-74bdaa997dcb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174738}] :question/id #uuid "498a8787-d5a7-5977-8e3c-6512904632e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-212151" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{1}{x - 2} = \\frac{2}{x + 4}$.$x =$\n{{field-1}}"}]}
